[Event "Benoni for white: the setup and the b5 gambit"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/qmydlUec/CNGCH7Oi"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis"]

{ Today you meet the Benoni with white. Take the space, then punish the pawn grab. [%cal Yd2d4] }
1. d4 { Queen pawn first. You grab the middle before he is awake. } (1. e4 { A fine move, but a whole different world. Today you play the queen pawn. } 1... c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 { That is the Sicilian, and another study entirely. }) (1. c4 { Also fine. The english usually walks back into the same place. } 1... Nf6 2. d4 c5 3. d5 e6 { The same Benoni, through a different door. }) 1... Nf6 { He develops and eyes your centre. Now the other pawn to c4. [%cal Yc2c4] } 2. c4 { Two big pawns side by side. That is the space you want. [%csl Gd4,Gc4] } (2. Nf3 { A good move, and it usually transposes. But we want c4 in first. } 2... c5 3. d5 e6 4. c4 exd5 5. cxd5 { You are home anyway. }) 2... c5 { The Benoni. He hits your centre from the side. [%csl Rc5] } 3. d5 { Push right past him. Now his pawn on c5 bites on nothing. [%csl Gd5][%cal Gd4d5] } (3. Nf3 { Reasonable. But pushing to d5 gives you far more space. } 3... cxd4 4. Nxd4 e6 5. e3 Nc6 { A normal game, and your space is gone. }) (3. Nc3 { This lets him take on d4 and free his game. } 3... cxd4 4. Qxd4 Nc6 5. Qd3 e6 { His pieces come out hitting your queen. }) (3. e3 { Solid but modest. He trades on d4 and stands fine. } 3... cxd4 4. exd4 d5 5. Nc3 Nc6 { Dead level. }) (3. dxc5 { You want to keep hold of the centre, not hand it back. } 3... e6 4. Be3 Qa5+ 5. Nc3 Bxc5 { He takes the pawn back with a free move. }) 3... e6 { The modern Benoni. If he plays b5 or e5 instead, that is another opening. [%cal Yb1c3] } 4. Nc3 { Develop, and put a guard on the pawn at d5. [%csl Gd5] } (4. dxe6 { This hands the centre straight back. Your proud pawn on d5 is gone. } 4... fxe6 5. Nf3 Nc6 6. Bf4 d5 { He owns the middle now. }) (4. Nf3 { Perfectly playable. But the knight on c3 minds d5 first. } 4... exd5 5. cxd5 d6 6. Nc3 g6 { The same thing, one move later. }) (4. Bg5 { Playable. But the bishop gets chased and you lose time. } 4... d6 5. Nc3 Be7 6. Bf4 Qa5 { Her majesty is annoying. }) 4... exd5 { The trade he always makes. Take back with the c pawn. [%cal Yc4d5] } 5. cxd5 { Correct. That pawn on d5 is a spear in his position. [%csl Gd5] } (5. Nxd5 { This trades off your best pawn and helps him out. } 5... Nxd5 6. cxd5 d6 7. Nf3 Be7 { He is completely fine. }) (5. e3 { Too slow. He takes on c4 and your big centre melts. } 5... dxc4 6. Bxc4 Nc6 7. Nge2 Ne5 { His knights are having a party. }) 5... d6 { He builds the Benoni wall. Now claim the whole centre with the e pawn. [%cal Ye2e4] } 6. e4 { Pawns on d5 and e4. That is real space. [%csl Ge4,Gd5] } (6. Nf3 { A fair move. But we want the big pawn centre first. } 6... g6 7. Nd2 Bg7 8. e4 O-O { You got there, after a detour. }) (6. Bg5 { Playable, but the bishop has nothing to pin yet. } 6... Be7 7. e4 a6 8. Nf3 b5 { He is already busy on the queen side. }) 6... g6 { He wants the long diagonal. Find a move that shuts his light bishop out. [%csl Rc8] } 7. h3 { A tiny move with a big job. No piece of his may ever use g4. [%csl Gg4][%cal Rf6g4,Rc8g4] } (7. f4 { The sharpest try, and a fine plan if you know it. It gets tricky fast. } 7... Bg7 8. Bb5+ Nfd7 9. a4 O-O { Great fun, but you have to know the details. }) (7. Nf3 { Also good. But now his bishop can land on g4. } 7... a6 8. Be2 Bg7 9. a4 Bg4 { There it is, the piece you wanted to keep out. }) (7. Be2 { Fine and solid. Our plan wants this bishop on d3. } 7... Bg7 8. Nf3 a6 9. O-O O-O { Playable, but g4 is still open to him. }) (7. Be3 { Also good. And you usually end up playing h3 anyway. } 7... Bg7 8. h3 O-O 9. Nf3 Re8 { The same position, a different order. }) (7. Bd3 { Defensive and fair. Remember the square, the bishop belongs there later. } 7... Bg7 8. Nge2 a6 9. a4 Nbd7 { Playable, but g4 is still free. }) (7. Bg5 { Fair. But the bishop bites on granite and can be pushed away. } 7... Bg7 8. Nf3 a6 9. a4 Bg4 { Both his bishops are busy and yours is not. }) (7. Bb5+ { Reasonable. But it helps him trade his worst piece. } 7... Bd7 8. a4 Bg7 9. Nf3 Bxb5 { His bad bishop is gone for your good one. }) 7... Bg7 { Now just develop. Knight to f3. [%cal Yg1f3] } 8. Nf3 { Out it comes, watching e5 and g5. } (8. a4 { Also strong. It stops b5 before he even thinks of it. } 8... O-O 9. Bd3 Re8 10. Nge2 b6 { A good game, just a different route. }) (8. Be3 { Just as good. The knight first keeps things simple. } 8... O-O 9. Nf3 Re8 10. Bd3 Nxe4 { Careful, that knight jump is a real idea. }) (8. Bd3 { You play this next move anyway. Nothing wrong with it. } 8... O-O 9. a4 Re8 10. Nge2 b6 { The same kind of position. }) (8. Bb5+ { It looks active, but he blocks and trades your good bishop. } 8... Bd7 9. a4 O-O 10. Bd3 Qa5 { You lost time with the bishop. }) (8. Bf4 { Keep that idea for later, when d6 is a real target. } 8... O-O 9. Bd3 Re8 10. Nge2 a6 { Playable, but the knight wanted f3. }) 8... O-O { He castles. Bring the bishop to d3, where it minds the pawn on e4. [%cal Yf1d3] } 9. Bd3 { The bishop guards e4 and looks across at his king. [%csl Ge4] } (9. Be3 { Just as good. But on d3 the bishop also guards the pawn on e4. } 9... Re8 10. Bd3 Nxe4 11. Nxe4 c4 { You end up on d3 anyway. }) (9. Nd2 { A good regrouping, heading for c4. It is slower though. } 9... Re8 10. Be2 Na6 11. O-O Nc7 { Playable, but the knight left f3. }) (9. Qc2 { Solid. The bishop on d3 does the same job and develops. } 9... Re8 10. Be3 Qe7 11. Nd2 Nxe4 { You still had to move that bishop. }) (9. Bg5 { Let us bring this bishop out later, so you can castle first. } 9... Re8 10. Bd3 c4 11. Bc2 b5 { A move spent, and you played d3 in the end. }) (9. Be2 { A fine square, but not the best one here. } 9... b5 10. Bxb5 Nxe4 11. Nxe4 Qa5+ 12. Nfd2 Qxb5 { He wins the piece straight back. }) (9. Bf4 { It hits d6, but it also frees his knight. } 9... Re8 10. Nd2 Nxe4 11. Ncxe4 f5 { He breaks out. }) (9. Bb5 { The bishop is loose out there. Watch the check on a5. } 9... Nxe4 10. Nxe4 Qa5+ 11. Nc3 Bxc3+ 12. bxc3 Qxb5 { You are simply a pawn down. }) (9. Bc4 { The pawn on d5 needs no help, and the bishop gets chased. } 9... Re8 10. Bd3 c4 11. Bxc4 Nxe4 { Two moves lost and the centre pawn with them. }) 9... b5 { He gives a pawn to blow open the queen side. Take it, and take with the knight. [%csl Rb5] } 10. Nxb5 { The knight grabs it, and from b5 it stares at d6. [%cal Gb5d6] } (10. O-O { Castling is safe. But if you do not take, he simply rolls forward. } 10... b4 11. Na4 Ba6 12. Re1 Bxd3 13. Qxd3 Qd7 { He owns the whole queen side. }) (10. Bxb5 { You can take with the bishop. But then his knight jump really works. } 10... Nxe4 11. Nxe4 Qa5+ 12. Nfd2 Qxb5 { The check on a5 wins the bishop back and you are only level. }) 10... Nxe4 { He keeps giving. This one is a real mistake. Take the knight. [%csl Re4] } 11. Bxe4 { The only way to take, and you are a whole piece up. } (11. O-O { Why did you leave a free knight sitting there? Now he keeps it. } 11... Re8 12. Re1 a6 13. Nc3 Nxc3 14. bxc3 { The piece is gone and the game is level again. }) (11. Bf4 { The knight on e4 is free. Grab it before anything else. } 11... a6 12. Na3 Qf6 13. Bc1 Nc3 { His knight is a monster now. }) 11... Re8 { He pins the bishop against your king. Defend it with the knight from g5. [%cal Re8e1,Yf3g5] } 12. Ng5 { The knight covers e4, and it eyes e6 and f7 as well. [%csl Ge4,Ge6,Gf7] } (12. O-O { The pin is gone, but so is your bishop. } 12... Rxe4 13. Nc3 Re8 14. Bg5 Qd7 { You handed the piece straight back. }) (12. Nd2 { This defends the bishop too, but it is passive. } 12... Ba6 13. a4 Qa5 14. Nxd6 Nd7 { Messy, and your grip is gone. }) (12. Qe2 { Now the queen is pinned as well, and the check on a5 lands. } 12... Ba6 13. a4 Qa5+ 14. Kd1 Bxb5 { Your king is stuck in the middle. }) (12. Qc2 { The bishop is still hanging to that rook. } 12... Bf5 13. Nd2 Bxe4 14. Nxe4 f5 { The piece goes back. }) 12... Qa5+ { A check, and it hits the knight on b5 too. Only one move saves everything. [%cal Ra5b5,Ra5e1] } 13. Nc3 { Block with the knight. It guards itself, and the extra piece stays. } (13. Kf1 { Running costs the knight on b5 and your castling. } 13... Qxb5+ 14. Kg1 Nd7 15. Bc2 Qb4 { The extra piece has vanished. }) (13. Qd2 { The queen block drops the knight on b5. } 13... Qxb5 14. a4 Qc4 15. Kd1 Nd7 { You are worse now. }) (13. Bd2 { The bishop block drops it as well. } 13... Qxb5 14. Qf3 f5 15. Bc3 Bxc3+ { Everything falls apart. }) 13... h6 { He kicks the knight. Do not go back. Jump in to e6. [%cal Yg5e6] } 14. Ne6 { Cheeky. If he takes with the f pawn, you castle and the bishop crashes into g6. [%csl Rg8] } (14. O-O { Safe, but now the knight on g5 is simply lost. } 14... hxg5 15. Re1 Qd8 16. Bf3 Rxe1+ { A piece for nothing. }) (14. Bd2 { This also hands him the knight on g5. } 14... hxg5 15. O-O Qd8 16. Bc2 g4 { Given back. }) 14... Bxc3+ { He grabs the knight with check. Take back with the b pawn. [%cal Yb2c3] } 15. bxc3 { The only good recapture. Your king still has a home. } (15. Bd2 { This trade lets everything come off, and your extra piece with it. } 15... Bxd2+ 16. Qxd2 Qxd2+ 17. Kxd2 fxe6 { All traded, and your edge is gone. }) (15. Kf1 { Running is safe, but the bishop returns to g7 and you are worse. } 15... Bg7 16. Kg1 Nd7 17. Bd2 Qb6 { You lost the thread. }) 15... Qxc3+ { Another check. Only one square keeps you on top. } 16. Bd2 { The bishop blocks, and your queen is minding it. [%cal Gd1d2] } (16. Qd2 { Trading queens is playable, but it lets him off the hook. } 16... Qxd2+ 17. Kxd2 fxe6 18. f4 e5 { Far less than you had. }) (16. Kf1 { Now the rook in the corner falls with the queen. } 16... Qxa1 17. g3 Nd7 18. Qc2 Nf6 { A whole rook gone. }) 16... Qc4 { He centralises and eyes your bishop. Offer him the trade of queens. [%cal Yd1e2] } 17. Qe2 { Lovely. Queens come off and you walk into a winning ending, a piece for two pawns. } (17. Qf3 { Also strong. But on e2 you offer the trade and simplify. } 17... Bxe6 18. Rc1 Bg4 19. Rxc4 Bxf3 { Still good, just much messier. }) (17. Bf3 { Too passive. He takes the knight and the pressure is over. } 17... fxe6 18. Rc1 Qb5 19. Rb1 Qc4 { You let him breathe. }) (17. f3 { Slow. He takes on e6 and only a small edge is left. } 17... fxe6 18. Be3 Bb7 19. Rc1 Qb4+ { Much less than you deserved. }) 1-0


