{[%eval +1.48] He throws the f pawn forward at your bishop. Who is really in danger here?} 1... f5 {[%eval +2.22] He attacks the bishop, and opens his own king doing it.} 2. O-O {[%cal Rg8g8]Castle. Let him take the bishop. His king is far more naked than yours.}  (2. Qa4 {The queen wanders and he consolidates.}  Bd7 3. Qc4 Bxb5 4. Qxb5 Nd7 {Much less than you had.})  (2. Qb3 {Also slow. He picks the bishop off in comfort.}  h6 3. Ne6 Bxe6 4. dxe6 Qb6 {You gave away most of it.})  (2. Qc2 {The bishop is hanging and now the pawn takes it for free.}  fxe4 3. O-O Na6 4. Nxe4 Bf5 {Level.})  (2. Qe2 {Same story, and the queen is awkward on e2.}  fxe4 3. O-O e3 4. Ne6 Bxe6 {You threw it away.}) fxe4 {He grabs it. Now bring the last piece out with a threat.} 3. Bf4 {[%cal Rd6d6]Straight at d6, and the rook comes to e1 next.}  (3. Re1 {Also strong, and it wins the pawn back.}  Qb6 4. a4 Bf5 5. Bf4 Be5 {A fine game for you, just slower.})  (3. Ne6 {Do not trade your best attacker for his bishop.}  Bxe6 4. dxe6 Nc6 5. e7 Qd7 {Most of the pull has gone.})  (3. Qb3 {The queen alone will not do it.}  h6 4. Ne6 Bxe6 5. dxe6 Qb6 {Only a small edge.}) Be5 {He offers the trade to calm things down. Bring the queen up instead.} 4. Qd2 {Every piece is pointing at his king now, and the pawn on e4 will drop.}  (4. Bxe5 {Trading is fine, but the queen move keeps more life in it.}  Rxe5 5. f4 exf3 6. Nxf3 Rf5 {Still very good, just simpler.})  (4. Be3 {Now he gets his bishop out with tempo.}  Bf5 5. g4 Bd7 6. Nc3 Bxc3 {Much of the attack has gone.})  (4. Bd2 {Too passive. He unwinds.}  Bf5 5. f4 Bxb2 6. Rb1 Bf6 {You let him breathe.}) 1-0

