{[%eval +1.41] Instead of the pawn kick he plays the bishop to a6. Your king cannot castle now.} 1... Ba6 {[%eval +1.21] The bishop stops you castling short. Find a plan for your king.} 2. Qc2 {[%eval +1.12] [%cal Ge1c1]The queen steps aside so the rooks can talk and the king can go long.}  (2. f3 {Also good, giving the king a square on f2.}  Qb4 3. a3 Qd4 4. a4 h6 {Still clearly better for you.})  (2. Bd2 {Solid, but the queen wanted c2 first.}  Nd7 3. Qc2 Ne5 4. O-O-O f5 {Less than you could have had.})  (2. Qd2 {The queen blocks her own bishop.}  Nd7 3. Kd1 Rab8 4. Bc2 Ne5 {Only a small edge left.})  (2. Qf3 {The queen is loose here and he swaps everything off.}  f5 3. Kd1 Bxc3 4. Qxc3 Qxc3 {All level.}) f5 {[%eval +1.19] He hits the bishop again. Finish developing.} 3. Bd2 {[%eval +1.35] Everything is defended, and the king is ready to run to the queen side.}  (3. f3 {Passive. The bishop gets shut in behind its own pawns.}  fxe4 4. Nxe4 Bc4 5. Kf2 Na6 {Most of your edge has gone.})  (3. f4 {This weakens the squares around your own king.}  fxe4 4. Nxe4 Nd7 5. Kf2 Nf6 {You are drifting.})  (3. Ne6 {The knight is worth more where it stands.}  Nc6 4. Nxg7 Rxe4+ 5. Be3 Nb4 {Now you are worse.})  (3. Bf4 {The bishop is loose on f4 and he wins the pawn.}  fxe4 4. O-O-O Nd7 5. Ngxe4 Rxe4 {Given back.}) Qb6 {[%eval +1.50] The queen steps off the pin line. Now switch wings.}  (3... fxe4 {[%eval +2.59] }  4. Ncxe4 {[%eval +2.47] }  (4. Ngxe4 {[%eval -0.50] }  ) Qb6 {[%eval +2.80] }) 4. Qa4 {[%eval +1.69] [%cal Ga4a6]The queen swings across and his loose queen side falls apart.}  (4. Ne6 {[%eval +1.15] Trading the proud knight lets him off.}  Nc6 {[%eval +1.19] } 5. Qb3 Nd4 6. Qxb6 axb6 {Much simpler for him.})  (4. O-O-O {Safe, but he takes the pawn on e4 first.}  fxe4 5. Rhe1 Nd7 6. Na4 Qb7 {Only a small edge.})  (4. Na4 {The knight goes offside and the queen escapes.}  Qb7 5. O-O-O fxe4 6. Nxe4 Qxd5 {He is back in it.}) 1-0

