{[%eval +0.72] He does not gamble. He plays a6 and prepares b5. Here is the real Benoni plan.} 1... a6 {[%eval +1.13] [%cal Ya2a4,Ra6b5]He is getting ready for b5. Stop it before it starts, with your own a pawn.} 2. a4 {[%eval +1.12] [%cal Gb5b5]Now b5 is off the menu for good. That is the whole idea.}  (2. O-O {Safe, but he gets b5 in and takes over the queen side.}  b5 3. Bf4 Qe7 4. Qd2 Nbd7 {He has the play you wanted to stop.})  (2. Bf4 {The bishop is well placed, but b5 comes anyway.}  b5 3. O-O Qe7 4. Qd2 Nbd7 {You are only a little better.})  (2. a3 {One square too few. He plays b5 with tempo.}  b5 3. Bf4 Qe7 4. O-O Nbd7 {The a pawn wanted a4.})  (2. Bg5 {It looks active, but he is faster on the wing.}  b5 3. O-O Re8 4. a3 Nbd7 {His pawns are rolling.}) Nbd7 {[%eval +1.22] [%cal Ye1g1]He develops. Time to tuck the king away.} 3. O-O {[%eval +1.09] King safe. Now improve the pieces.}  (3. Be3 {Also fine. But castling first is never wrong.}  Ne8 4. O-O Ne5 5. Nxe5 Bxe5 {A good game, just slower.})  (3. Bf4 {Playable. But he hits the bishop with the c pawn at once.}  c4 4. Bc2 Ne5 5. Nxe5 Nh5 {A bit loose.})  (3. Bd2 {The bishop does nothing on d2.}  Re8 4. O-O c4 5. Bc2 Nc5 {His knight found a lovely square.})  (3. Bg5 {The bishop gets chased around.}  Re8 4. O-O h6 5. Bf4 Nh5 {Time lost.}) Re8 {[%eval +1.17] [%cal Yc1f4,Rd6d6]The rook eyes your pawn on e4. Now aim a bishop at his weak pawn on d6.} 4. Bf4 {[%eval +1.13] [%cal Gf4d6]Straight at d6. If he ever pushes to c4, just step back to c2.}  (4. Be3 {Also good. But d6 is the sore spot, not c5.}  Nh5 5. Be2 Bxc3 6. bxc3 Rxe4 {He got the pawn back.})  (4. Re1 {Solid, but the bishop wanted f4 first.}  Ne5 5. Nxe5 Rxe5 6. f4 Re8 {Playable, and less.})  (4. Bd2 {The bishop is doing nothing there.}  Rb8 5. Re1 Ne5 6. Nxe5 Rxe5 {He is comfortable.})  (4. Qe2 {The queen blocks the rook and he grabs space.}  Nh5 5. g4 Nhf6 6. Bf4 h5 {Your king side is loose.}) Nh5 {[%eval +1.18] [%cal Yf4h2]He offers the bishop trade. Do not take, and do not run far.} 5. Bh2 {[%eval +1.28] [%cal Gh2d6]Tucked away, still glaring down at d6.}  (5. Bg5 {The bishop is chased again from here.}  Bf6 6. Bd2 Ne5 7. Nxe5 Bxe5 {You lost the good diagonal.})  (5. Bd2 {Too far back. The bishop is buried.}  Rb8 6. Re1 Ne5 7. Be2 Nxf3+ {Nothing to show for it.})  (5. Bxd6 {Greedy. His pieces come alive and your pawn on e4 falls.}  Qb6 6. e5 Bxe5 7. Bxe5 Nxe5 {Only a shade better.})  (5. Be3 {Now the knights come to e5 with tempo.}  Ne5 6. Be2 Nxf3+ 7. Bxf3 Nf6 {He has untangled.}) Ne5 {[%eval +1.20] [%cal Yf3e5]He offers a trade in the middle. Accept it.} 6. Nxe5 {[%eval +1.17] Take. His pawn structure has to change.}  (6. Be2 {Now he trades on f3 and gets what he wanted.}  Nxf3+ 7. Bxf3 Nf6 8. Qd2 b6 {Comfortable for him.})  (6. Re1 {Same problem. He trades on his terms.}  Nxf3+ 7. Qxf3 Bd4 8. Rab1 b6 {His bishop found a home.})  (6. Bxe5 {Never trade this bishop for that knight.}  Rxe5 7. Nxe5 Bxe5 8. Ne2 Qh4 {His pieces are suddenly active.})  (6. a5 {Good idea, wrong moment. Take the knight first.}  Nxd3 7. Qxd3 f5 8. exf5 Bxf5 {He freed himself.}) dxe5 {[%eval +1.20] [%cal Ya4a5]His weak pawn on d6 is gone, but so is all his play. Push the a pawn.} 7. a5 {[%eval +1.29] [%cal Gb6b6]The pawn freezes his whole queen side. This is a dream Benoni for white.}  (7. Re1 {Fine, but the pawn on a5 is the move that really bites.}  b6 8. Bf1 Qg5 9. Kh1 Qf6 {He is holding.})  (7. Qd2 {Reasonable, and slower.}  b6 8. Kh1 Qf6 9. f3 Nf4 {His knight found f4.})  (7. Kh1 {Too quiet. He gets b6 in and unwinds.}  b6 8. Qd2 Qf6 9. f3 Nf4 {He is fine.})  (7. Qc2 {Same idea, one move too slow.}  b6 8. Rfe1 Nf4 9. Bf1 Bd7 {He has equalised the play.}) 1-0

